      "answer": "The customer whose issue started after the 2026-02-20 taxonomy rollout is BlueHarbor Logistics. They reported search relevance degradation starting from the taxonomy change on 2026-02-20, with saved searches returning low-relevance hits and duplicates, increasing manual triage time to about 6 hours per day [art_8b0063fbb3cb, art_bd3560dfe194].\n\nThe proof plan proposed to BlueHarbor Logistics to get them comfortable with renewal includes:\n\n- A short-term mitigation involving updating index weighting and adding a translation/mapping layer that maps new taxonomy terms to historical query tokens for the top 20 saved searches.\n- A 7-10 business day A/B test in staging starting 2026-03-22, with 5 business days to tune and 2 business days for validation.\n- Validation criteria requiring a top-5 correct hit rate of at least 80% for prioritized queries, no regression in EN-RULES-ENGINE suppression logic, and incident correlation latency under 200ms.\n- If validation passes, a 1-day production rollout followed by a 72-hour monitoring window.\n- A 90-day fixed-price professional services engagement at $48,000 to implement the tuning, build the mapping layer, and run the proof, with a 30% discount if BlueHarbor concurrently signs for Orchestrator expansion.\n- A conditional credit clause offering a one-quarter invoice credit prorated against the renewal if the proof does not meet the agreed accuracy uplift within 10 business days [art_bd3560dfe194, art_0bccc580184e].",
